d Body Applications","url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s326571/11-appendix-2-approved-admitted-body-applications.pdf","committee":"Pensions Board","date":"14/07/2026","key":false}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Scrutiny Board","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Council","time":"5.45 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Adults Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Pensions Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Economy and Growth Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.45 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-01","committee":"Health Scrutiny Panel","time":"1.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-07","committee":"Community Safety, Residents and Environmental Services Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Pensions Board","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Governance and Ethics Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Scrutiny Board","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Children,Young and People Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Resources and Equality Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Audit and Risk Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Wolverhampton repays \u00a317m in loans amid sharp rate hike demands","lead":"Wolverhampton Council rejected proposals to increase rates on three bank loans totaling \u00a317m and chose instead to repay them in full. LOBO loans are bank loans where lenders can propose new terms on set dates. Between July 2025 and January 2026, a \u00a37m Commerzbank loan came with a proposed rate of 6.19%, compared with its existing 3.60%; two \u00a35m Dexia loans proposed 6.21% and 6.26%, against existing rates of 4.26% and 4.29%. According to the Treasury Management Annual Report, this refinancing required \u00a317m in cash but protected the council from substantially higher day-to-day running costs.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Appendix A Treasury Management Annual Report 2526 Activity Monitoring Quarter One 2627","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329075/329075.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Resources and Equality Scrutiny Panel","paper_title":"Appendix A Treasury Management Annual Report 2526 Activity Monitoring Quarter One 2627","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329075/329075.pdf","headline":"Three LOBO loans totaling \u00a317m were called in 2025-2026; the council repaid them at par rather than accept new rates of 6.19-6.26%","detail":"A \u00a37m Commerzbank LOBO was called on 31 July 2025 with a new rate proposed of 6.19% versus the existing 3.60%. Two \u00a35m Dexia LOBOs were called on 26 and 30 January 2026 with proposed rates of 6.21% and 6.26% versus existing rates of 4.26% and 4.29%. The council rejected all three proposals and repaid the loans. These calls represent a refinancing and liquidity event, requiring the council to secure \u00a317m in cash to repay at par.","category":"borrowing","matters":"LOBO calls materialize the refinancing risk embedded in the council's debt portfolio. The high rates offered (60-173 basis points above existing rates) show market stress on LOBO lending at the time. A lender cares whether the council can refinance when forced to do so; the fact that the council had the liquidity to repay \u00a317m at par demonstrates this capacity, but it also shows the council is exposed to repricing risk when the lender chooses to exercise options.","figures":"\u00a317m total LOBO calls: Commerzbank \u00a37m (3.60% to 6.19%), Dexia \u00a35m (4.26% to 6.21%), Dexia \u00a35m (4.29% to 6.26%)","quote":"During 2025-2026 the following LOBOs were 'called' and the Council decided to reject the proposal and the loans were repaid:","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Our Future Council - Delivering Our Services - Performance and Budget Monitoring 2026-2027 and Our Future Council 2.0 - Investing in Our Services","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s327176/327176.pdf","headline":"Pay award settlement at 3.3% exceeds 3% budget provision; gap must be funded from contingencies or reserves","detail":"Budget included 3% pay provision but national employers offered 3.3% in March 2026, accepted for chief officers in June 2026. Negotiations continue for other pay grades. Any increase above 3% requires funding from corporate contingencies, in-year efficiencies or reserves, with recurrent impact built into future MTFS.","category":"overspend","matters":"Pay cost overruns reduce available resources; multiple pay grades settling above budget is a material risk to the balanced budget position","figures":"3% budgeted provision; 3.3% settled for chief officers; negotiations ongoing for other grades","quote":"the budget includes provision for a 3% pay award. In March 2026 National Employers on behalf of local authorities made a full and final offer of 3.3% for pay award for the 2026-2027 financial year.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Our Future Council - Delivering Our Services - Performance and Budget Monitoring 2026-2027 and Our Future Council 2.0 - Investing in Our Services","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s327176/327176.pdf","headline":"DSG deficit of \u00a314.1 million excluded from accounts via statutory override, but override expires 31 March 2028 and government grant coverage unclear","detail":"Cumulative DSG deficit of \u00a314.1 million is currently hidden from accounts via statutory override, extended only to 31 March 2028. Government's promised High Needs Stability Grant may cover up to 90% of deficit but eligibility and actual amount remain unknown. Balance would remain with council.","category":"other","matters":"This is a contingent liability; when override ends, if grant doesn't cover full amount, council could face \u00a314.1 million call on reserves or emergency savings mid-MTFS period","figures":"\u00a314.1 million cumulative net DSG deficit as at 31 March 2026; statutory override extended to 31 March 2028; High Needs Stability Grant up to 90% of deficit but amount unknown","quote":"The cumulative net DSG deficit to 31 March 2026 totalled \u00a314.1 million. The statutory override allowing DSG deficits to be excluded from the Council's wider financial position has been extended to 31 March 2028.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Our Future Council - Delivering Our Services - Performance and Budget Monitoring 2026-2027 and Our Future Council 2.0 - Investing in Our Services","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s327176/327176.pdf","headline":"Savings target delivery at risk: some services indicating slippage or non-delivery against \u00a38.4 million 2026-2027 savings target","detail":"While majority of services are on track, a small number of the \u00a38.4 million approved savings targets are indicating potential slippage or non-delivery. Council has \u00a33.3 million corporate contingency held to cover shortfalls. Some areas showing overachievement, but delivery remains closely monitored.","category":"savings","matters":"Failure to deliver planned savings would immediately worsen the medium-term position and increase reliance on reserves or emergency service cuts","figures":"\u00a38.4 million total 2026-2027 savings targets; \u00a33.3 million corporately held contingency for slippage","quote":"A small number of these savings targets are indicating some potential slippage or non-delivery. However, early indications also show that in some areas there is potential overachievement of savings delivery.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Our Future Council - Delivering Our Services - Performance and Budget Monitoring 2026-2027 and Our Future Council 2.0 - Investing in Our Services","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s327176/327176.pdf","headline":"Structural budget deficit of \u00a33.8 million forecast for 2027-2028, rising to \u00a314.8 million in 2028-2029, despite improvement from prior projections","detail":"Despite progress from Our Future Council programme, the council faces significant medium-term funding gaps. The deficit has improved from \u00a323.5 million modelled the previous year, but a \u00a314.8 million structural shortfall by 2028-2029 signals the council will need to find substantial additional savings or service reductions. This assumes council tax increases to referendum limits.","category":"savings","matters":"A \u00a314.8 million structural deficit indicates the current financial model is not yet sustainable and further major service changes or income growth will be required","figures":"\u00a33.8 million projected deficit 2027-2028; \u00a314.8 million projected deficit 2028-2029; improvement from \u00a323.5 million previously modelled","quote":"the Council is now faced with a projected budget deficit of \u00a33.8 million for 2027-2028 (compared to \u00a323.5 million when modelled in the previous year's budget and MTFS report), rising to \u00a314.8 million in 2028-2029.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Wolverhampton","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-22","since_iso":"2026-08-23","distress":false,"headline":"Three LOBO loans totaling \u00a317m were called in 2025-2026; the council repaid them at par rather than accept new rates of 6.19-6.26%","lead":"LOBO calls materialize the refinancing risk embedded in the council's debt portfolio. The high rates offered (60-173 basis points above existing rates) show market stress on LOBO lending at the time. A lender cares whether the council can refinance when\u2026","also":[],"watch":"Scrutiny Board on 2026-09-22","n_findings":1,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix A Treasury Management Annual Report 2526 Activity Monitoring Quarter One 2627","top_paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329075/329075.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":81,"news_drafts_total":1}}